  "summary": "Cast bronze plaques and sculptures from the Benin Kingdom, depicting royal court life and religious ceremonies.",
  "description": "Featuring intricate relief work with detailed figures, patterns, and hierarchical compositions, these artworks were created using the lost-wax casting technique. They served to glorify the Oba (king) and communicate royal power and spiritual authority.",
  "culturalContext": "The Benin Bronzes — Edo Kingdom of Benin's cast brass plaques, heads and figures (13th-19th c.) — documented court history with lost-wax mastery: the 1897 British punitive expedition's looting scattered 3,000+ pieces to world museums; Germany's 2022 returns and Smithsonian transfers made repatriation precedent. The bronzes' court-narrative reliefs (obas, Portuguese traders, court officials) are Africa's most studied sculptural archive and Nigeria's central heritage claim.",
